1
00:00:00,800 --> 00:00:04,030
From an engineering point of view, the most important

2
00:00:04,030 --> 00:00:07,430
question about a material is how it responds to load.

3
00:00:07,430 --> 00:00:12,757
Almost every part, even an electronic substrate, has some mechanical job to do.

4
00:00:15,877 --> 00:00:21,153
The key properties are strength, hardness, stiffness, ductility and toughness.

5
00:00:21,153 --> 00:00:24,635
We measure them with standardized tests, maintained by A S T

6
00:00:24,635 --> 00:00:28,225
M, so everyone measures the same thing in the same way.

7
00:00:28,645 --> 00:00:32,805
Loads come in four forms: tension, compression, shear and torsion.

8
00:00:32,805 --> 00:00:35,546
Pulling, squeezing, sliding and twisting.

9
00:00:39,346 --> 00:00:44,645
A thick wire carries more load than a thin one before it breaks, so we normalize.

10
00:00:44,645 --> 00:00:49,484
Engineering stress is the force divided by the original cross sectional area.

11
00:00:49,903 --> 00:00:54,444
Engineering strain is the change in length divided by the original length.

12
00:00:54,444 --> 00:00:55,733
It has no units.

13
00:00:56,154 --> 00:00:59,312
In shear, tau is force over area too, and the shear

14
00:00:59,312 --> 00:01:02,203
strain gamma is the tangent of the shear angle.

15
00:01:02,623 --> 00:01:05,796
Using the original area gives engineering stress.

16
00:01:05,796 --> 00:01:09,309
Using the actual, shrinking area gives true stress.

17
00:01:13,009 --> 00:01:17,189
At small loads, deformation is elastic, which means reversible.

18
00:01:17,189 --> 00:01:19,815
The bonds between atoms stretch, and when you

19
00:01:19,815 --> 00:01:22,763
unload, the atoms return to where they were.

20
00:01:23,182 --> 00:01:27,959
Like a spring obeying Hooke's law, stress and strain are proportional.

21
00:01:27,959 --> 00:01:30,082
Sigma equals E times epsilon.

22
00:01:30,082 --> 00:01:35,507
E is Young's modulus, or the modulus of elasticity: the material's stiffness.

23
00:01:35,927 --> 00:01:40,262
Typical moduli: ceramics around three hundred gigapascals, steel two

24
00:01:40,262 --> 00:01:45,054
hundred seven, copper one hundred ten, and plastics only about three.

25
00:01:45,473 --> 00:01:47,249
Your lecture's example.

26
00:01:47,249 --> 00:01:52,578
A steel wire, zero point five five square millimeters in area and ten meters long,

27
00:01:52,578 --> 00:01:57,788
stretches one point six eight millimeters under seventeen point two four newtons.

28
00:01:58,208 --> 00:02:01,581
The stress is seventeen point two four over zero point

29
00:02:01,581 --> 00:02:05,454
five five: thirty one point three five megapascals.

30
00:02:05,454 --> 00:02:08,640
The strain is one point six eight over ten thousand

31
00:02:08,640 --> 00:02:13,137
millimeters: one point six eight times ten to the minus four.

32
00:02:13,557 --> 00:02:18,245
So E equals stress over strain: about one hundred eighty seven gigapascals.

33
00:02:18,245 --> 00:02:20,480
Quite reasonable for a steel wire.

34
00:02:24,280 --> 00:02:27,845
The modulus drops as temperature rises, because the atoms

35
00:02:27,845 --> 00:02:31,293
vibrate more and the bonds become easier to stretch.

36
00:02:31,713 --> 00:02:35,037
Some materials, like cast iron, concrete and many

37
00:02:35,037 --> 00:02:38,186
polymers, have a non linear elastic region.

38
00:02:38,186 --> 00:02:42,675
Then we use the tangent modulus, the slope at a chosen stress, or the

39
00:02:42,675 --> 00:02:47,282
secant modulus, the slope of the line from the origin to that point.

40
00:02:47,702 --> 00:02:51,725
And anelasticity: in some materials, elastic strain develops

41
00:02:51,725 --> 00:02:55,689
gradually after loading and recovers slowly after unloading.

42
00:02:55,689 --> 00:02:58,352
It is small in metals, but significant in

43
00:02:58,352 --> 00:03:02,020
polymers, where it is called viscoelastic behavior.

44
00:03:05,720 --> 00:03:09,662
Pull a bar along z, and it gets longer and thinner.

45
00:03:09,662 --> 00:03:12,079
Poisson's ratio measures that sideways

46
00:03:12,079 --> 00:03:16,212
contraction: nu equals minus epsilon x over epsilon z.

47
00:03:16,633 --> 00:03:20,476
Typical values are zero point two to zero point five.

48
00:03:20,476 --> 00:03:24,832
And for isotropic materials, E, the shear modulus G, and

49
00:03:24,832 --> 00:03:28,611
nu are linked: E equals two G times one plus nu.

50
00:03:32,410 --> 00:03:36,979
Beyond the elastic region, deformation becomes plastic: permanent.

51
00:03:36,979 --> 00:03:39,743
Let us run a full tensile test, and watch the

52
00:03:39,743 --> 00:03:42,733
specimen and its stress strain curve together.

53
00:03:43,153 --> 00:03:46,930
The straight elastic part ends at the proportional limit.

54
00:03:46,930 --> 00:03:50,767
Because that point is hard to pin down, the yield strength is

55
00:03:50,767 --> 00:03:54,485
defined with the zero point zero zero two offset method.

56
00:03:54,906 --> 00:03:57,541
Draw a line parallel to the elastic slope,

57
00:03:57,541 --> 00:04:00,749
starting at a strain of zero point zero zero two.

58
00:04:00,749 --> 00:04:04,702
Where it crosses the curve is the yield strength, sigma y.

59
00:04:05,122 --> 00:04:08,256
Some low carbon steels show a yield point instead: an

60
00:04:08,256 --> 00:04:11,774
upper yield point, followed by a drop to a lower one.

61
00:04:12,493 --> 00:04:15,835
The highest point on the curve is the tensile strength.

62
00:04:15,835 --> 00:04:18,368
After it, the deformation concentrates in a

63
00:04:18,368 --> 00:04:21,171
neck, and the specimen finally fractures.

64
00:04:25,072 --> 00:04:29,573
Ductility is how much plastic deformation happens before fracture.

65
00:04:29,573 --> 00:04:32,655
Measure it as percent elongation: final length

66
00:04:32,655 --> 00:04:36,046
minus original length, over original length.

67
00:04:36,046 --> 00:04:38,451
Or as percent reduction in area.

68
00:04:38,870 --> 00:04:43,174
A brittle material fractures at less than about five percent strain.

69
00:04:43,174 --> 00:04:47,593
Resilience is the energy absorbed elastically, and toughness is the total

70
00:04:47,593 --> 00:04:51,840
energy absorbed up to fracture: the whole area under the curve.

71
00:04:55,640 --> 00:04:57,954
Hardness is the resistance to localized

72
00:04:57,954 --> 00:05:01,276
plastic deformation: a small dent or scratch.

73
00:05:01,276 --> 00:05:04,717
An indenter is pressed into the surface under a controlled

74
00:05:04,717 --> 00:05:08,810
load, and the size or depth of the impression is measured.

75
00:05:09,230 --> 00:05:12,724
Brinell uses a hard ball, Rockwell measures the depth of

76
00:05:12,724 --> 00:05:16,626
penetration, and Vickers and Knoop use diamond pyramids.

77
00:05:16,626 --> 00:05:20,178
Softer materials leave larger, deeper impressions.

78
00:05:20,598 --> 00:05:24,648
Hardness tests are quick, cheap and nearly non destructive.

79
00:05:24,648 --> 00:05:28,293
And for many steels, the tensile strength in megapascals is

80
00:05:28,293 --> 00:05:31,996
roughly three point four five times the Brinell hardness.

81
00:05:35,796 --> 00:05:37,686
Here is the summary of topic eight.

82
00:05:38,106 --> 00:05:40,958
Stress is force over original area, and in the

83
00:05:40,958 --> 00:05:44,153
elastic region, stress equals E times strain.

84
00:05:44,573 --> 00:05:48,893
The modulus falls with temperature, and Poisson's ratio links the strains.

85
00:05:49,312 --> 00:05:52,244
Yield strength comes from the zero point zero zero two

86
00:05:52,244 --> 00:05:56,100
offset, then the tensile strength, necking and fracture.

87
00:05:56,520 --> 00:06:00,231
And ductility, toughness and hardness complete the picture.

88
00:06:00,231 --> 00:06:04,314
Next, why metals yield at all: dislocations and strengthening.
